   "revision": "d4175fd68ac7c5890afe24fb387a4ad76053a4be (last modified 2025-09-24); validation.parquet sha256 ab4399cbf3b13c0ab2d1bc8dcded6fdf015a860d48a1005b4f13c2bd58c19f06",
   "paper": "Thorat and Caines 2025, arXiv:2508.00619",
   "use": "Domains student_essays and abstracts only (news was not used). The 11 prompted LLMs (GPT-4o and 4o-mini, Claude 3.5 Haiku and Sonnet, Gemini 1.5 Flash and Pro, Llama 3.2 90B and 3.3 70B, Mistral Small and Large, DeepSeek-V3); the continued-pre-training small models (adversarial set) are excluded. Human student essays are from Ivy Panda and ELLIPSE; essays tagged ELL=True (ELLIPSE, English language learners) form the non-native group.",
   "caveats": "The card's MIT licence covers the corpus; the licences of its underlying human sources (Ivy Panda essays scraped from a website, the ELLIPSE corpus, arXiv abstracts) were not checked at their own sources, so texts stay local and are never published. The validation split was not used to train or test any Lectern model (the style model used the train and test splits)."
  },
  {
   "name": "Beemo (Benchmark of Expert-Edited Machine-generated Outputs)",
   "url": "https://huggingface.co/datasets/toloka/beemo",
   "licence": "MIT for the card and the expert edits. The card also states: human-written texts and prompts come from No Robots under CC-BY-NC-4.0; machine outputs and LLM edits follow the licences of the generating models (Llama, Gemma and others).",
   "revision": "9c014107fe9b85c4c784c1ce3a43b0b7b0a6d162 (last modified 2025-01-28)",
   "paper": "Artemova et al. 2024, arXiv:2411.04032 (NAACL 2025)",
   "use": "Per prompt: human reference (human_output) as human, model_output as AI (ten open-source instruction-tuned models, 2023-24), human_edits (expert annotators editing the machine output) as AI-edited by humans. Only the human-edits column is used for the edited class; the GPT-4o and Llama-3.1 edit columns are not used.",
   "caveats": "General writing (generation, rewriting, question answering, summaries), not academic coursework. Use here is local research evaluation, noncommercial."
  },
  {
   "name": "MixSet (LLM-as-a-Coauthor)",
   "url": "https://huggingface.co/datasets/ONE-Lab/MixSet (also github.com/Dongping-Chen/MixSet)",
   "licence": "NONE STATED: the Hugging Face card has no licence field and the GitHub repository has no LICENSE file. The paper only says all resources were used according to their licences. Texts are used locally only, are never committed or redistributed.",
   "revision": "b6063c77f660c2ef2e17461d85c24219a88f7d19 (last modified 2024-04-13); Mixset_test.json sha256 0f0f570ccb449b51afb75386047d2f67806e4d34918ab4cab80143799c4a0228, Mixset_train.json sha256 4fe988433e7e3242b55eda72ec242c17102ad0846d9b961f48395d3a6ebf269e",
   "paper": "Zhang et al. 2024, arXiv:2401.05952 (NAACL 2024 Findings)",
   "use": "Only the Human-Adapt rows (mixset_category MGT_polish_token and MGT_polish_sentence: machine text adapted by manual annotation) with 150-700 words. The AI-revised rows (GPT-4 or Llama 2 polishing, rewriting or completing) are model-edited text, a different class, and are not used. Humanize rows are LLM-added noise and are not used.",
   "caveats": "Only 18 such rows reach 150 words, all question-answer style (NarrativeQA and TruthfulQA answers), not academic writing."
  },
  {
   "name": "Liang et al. 2023 (ChatGPT detectors are biased against non-native English writers)",
   "url": "https://huggingface.co/datasets/WxWx/ChatGPT-Detector-Bias",
   "licence": "MIT (card metadata)",
   "revision": "ca6b48c8ce435b4750efc895d3c40bfab8bd7d9a (checked 2026-11-18; the texts were cached from this dataset by bench/fetch.js on 2026-11-15)",
   "paper": "Liang et al. 2023, Patterns, doi:10.1016/j.patter.2023.100779",
   "use": "Native student essays (human) and the GPT-3 and GPT-4 essays (AI) of 150-700 words.",
   "caveats": "None of the 91 TOEFL essays reaches 150 words (the longest is under 150), so they cannot enter this benchmark at the 150-word floor; the non-native group comes from DACTYL's ELL essays instead. The GPT-4-polished TOEFL essays are human text edited by a model and are a different class, not used. These texts were scored by Lectern's free measured index and style model earlier, never by the model review."
